## Initial Q&A
### Did any major health regulatory agency, such as the United States Food and Drug Administration (FDA) or the World Health Organization (WHO), approve or authorize Ivermectin as a treatment for COVID-19 on or before October 26, 2020?
Answer: No, according to the provided search result, the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) "has not approved the use of... ivermectin for treating or preventing COVID-19 in humans." The source further notes that "adequate clinical trials have not been done to prove ivermectin is effective" and there remains "not enough evidence to recommend ivermectin as a COVID-19 treatment."

Source URL: https://scdhec.gov/covid19/dangers-using-hydroxychloroquine-ivermectin-preventing-or-treating-covid-19

### What scientific evidence or clinical trial results existed as of October 26, 2020, regarding the efficacy of Ivermectin in reducing mortality or recovery time in COVID-19 patients?
Answer: As of October 2020, there was "insufficient evidence" and no reliable clinical trial data demonstrating that ivermectin could reduce mortality or recovery time in COVID-19 patients. Although an *in-vitro* study published in April 2020 showed that ivermectin dramatically reduced viral replication in a petri dish, experts warned that these results had not been replicated in humans or animals, and that the necessary dosage to achieve the same effect would be "very difficult or impossible to achieve in a person." Furthermore, while some proponents claimed high success rates using ivermectin combinations, infectious disease experts noted there was "no clinical evidence" to support those claims and described the existing observational data from overseas as "pretty poor quality."

Source URL: https://www1.racgp.org.au/newsgp/clinical/insufficient-evidence-to-currently-support-ivermec

### Were the early laboratory studies showing Ivermectin's antiviral activity against SARS-CoV-2 conducted in vitro (in a laboratory cell culture) or in vivo (in living human subjects) prior to October 26, 2020?
Answer: The early laboratory studies showing Ivermectin's antiviral activity against SARS-CoV-2 prior to October 26, 2020, were conducted *in vitro* (in laboratory cell cultures) rather than *in vivo* (in living human subjects). According to the April 2020 report, researchers "demonstrated that ivermectin can stop SARS-CoV-2 growth in cell cultures," and they cautioned that the next step was to "figure out now whether the dosage you can use it at in humans will be effective."

Source URL: https://www.drugtargetreview.com/news/59414/ivermectin-shows-activity-against-covid-19-in-cell-cultures/

### Did the concentration of Ivermectin required to inhibit SARS-CoV-2 in laboratory cell cultures exceed the maximum safe dosage of Ivermectin approved for human consumption?
Answer: Yes, the concentration of ivermectin required to inhibit SARS-CoV-2 in laboratory cell cultures significantly exceeded the approved human dosage. According to the search result, the concentration resulting in 50% inhibition (IC50; 2 µM) in vitro "was > 35× higher than the maximum plasma concentration (Cmax ) after oral administration of the approved dose of ivermectin," and plasma concentrations "do not reach the IC50 , even for a dose level 10× higher than the approved dose."

Source URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32378737/

