### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence demonstrates that as of October 26, 2020, there was no clinical evidence supporting the use of ivermectin as an effective treatment for COVID-19 in humans. The only study showing antiviral activity against SARS-CoV-2 was conducted *in vitro* (in cell cultures), and the concentration of ivermectin required to inhibit the virus was more than 35 times higher than the maximum safe dosage approved for human consumption. Consequently, major health regulatory agencies and medical organizations, including the FDA, WHO, and NIH, did not approve or recommend ivermectin for treating COVID-19, warning instead against its use outside of clinical trials due to the lack of established benefits and the risk of severe side effects, toxicity, or overdose.

### Conclusion
Based on the documented evidence, the claim that ivermectin is a treatment for coronavirus is explicitly contradicted by scientific data and major health authorities. At the time of the claim, there was no clinical proof of its efficacy in humans, and the drug concentrations required to inhibit the virus in laboratory settings far exceeded safe human limits. Because health organizations recommended against its use and confirmed that its benefits had not been established, the decision for this claim is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
As of October 2020, major health regulatory agencies like the FDA, WHO, and NIH had not approved or recommended ivermectin for COVID-19 due to a lack of clinical evidence, as documented by the [South Carolina Department of Health and Environmental Control](https://scdhec.gov/covid19/dangers-using-hydroxychloroquine-ivermectin-preventing-or-treating-covid-19) and [Spenta México](http://www.spentamexico.org/v15-n2/A2.15(2)1-30.pdf). While an early study reported by [Drug Target Review](https://www.drugtargetreview.com/news/59414/ivermectin-shows-activity-against-covid-19-in-cell-cultures/) showed antiviral activity *in vitro*, research published on [PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32378737/) revealed that the concentration required to inhibit the virus was over 35 times higher than the maximum safe dosage for humans. According to the [Royal Australian College of General Practitioners](https://www1.racgp.org.au/newsgp/clinical/insufficient-evidence-to-currently-support-ivermec), there was insufficient clinical evidence to support its efficacy in humans, and some key papers promoting it were later retracted due to falsified data. Furthermore, using unapproved dosages of ivermectin poses severe health risks, including toxicity and overdose, as detailed by [UW Medicine](https://newsroom.uw.edu/video-library/pharmacist-details-ivermectins-dangerous-snowball-effect). Therefore, the claim is refuted because scientific consensus and regulatory bodies explicitly advise against using ivermectin to treat COVID-19 due to safety risks and a lack of proven efficacy.